+# Proof: parse_date ISO 8601 with timezone
+
+## Change
+`calc.py:16` — `parse_date` now strips a trailing `Z` (UTC) and parses the first 10 characters as a date.
+
+```python
+def parse_date(s):
+    if s.endswith("Z"):
+        s = s[:-1]
+    return datetime.date.fromisoformat(s[:10])
+```
+
+## Verification
+
+```
+$ python3 -m unittest -v
+test_add (test_calc.CalcTest.test_add) ... ok
+test_mean (test_calc.CalcTest.test_mean) ... ok
+test_normalize (test_calc.CalcTest.test_normalize) ... ok
+test_parse_date (test_calc.CalcTest.test_parse_date) ... ok
+
+Ran 4 tests in 0.000s
+OK
+```
+
+```
+$ python3 -c "import calc; print(calc.parse_date('2026-01-02T03:04:05Z')); print(calc.parse_date('2026-01-02'))"
+2026-01-02
+2026-01-02
+```
+
+Both `parse_date('2026-01-02T03:04:05Z')` and `parse_date('2026-01-02')` return `datetime.date(2026, 1, 2)`.
